---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: 'About CircuitSimLab',
  description:
    'CircuitSimLab is a free, browser-based circuit simulator built on a real modified-nodal-analysis solver that runs DC, transient and AC analysis client-side.',
};

const principles = [
  {
    t: 'Every number is solved',
    d: 'No scripted animations or canned results. A modified-nodal-analysis solver — the same math SPICE uses — builds the conductance matrix and solves the circuit. What you read is what the analysis returns.',
  },
  {
    t: 'Fast by construction',
    d: 'The site is static HTML that loads instantly. The app is a single interactive canvas island, and the solver runs in a Web Worker so the interface never stutters while a transient sweep is grinding.',
  },
  {
    t: 'Your circuits stay yours',
    d: 'Projects are saved locally in your browser. Nothing is uploaded, there is no account, and a share link carries the whole circuit in the URL rather than on our servers.',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="About — CircuitSimLab"
  description="Why CircuitSimLab exists: a real browser-based circuit simulator with an actual MNA solver running DC, transient and AC analysis in a Web Worker. Build. Simulate. Understand."
  path="/about"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-line">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 grid-paper grid-paper-fade opacity-60" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="container-page relative py-16 md:py-24">
      <p class="tnum text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-accent">About</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-4xl font-semibold leading-[1.05] tracking-tight md:text-5xl">
        Build. Simulate. Understand.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg text-ink-2">
        CircuitSimLab is a real electronics lab that lives in a browser tab. You draw a schematic,
        press run, and a genuine solver works out the voltages and currents — then draws them back
        onto the wires you built.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Mission -->
  <section class="container-page py-16 md:py-20">
    <div class="doc max-w-3xl">
      <h2>Why we built it</h2>
      <p>
        Most "circuit simulators" you find in a browser are really diagrams that light up on cue.
        They look convincing until you change a resistor value and nothing moves the way physics
        says it should. We wanted the opposite: a tool that is honest about the math, quick to
        open, and pleasant enough that you reach for it before wiring up a breadboard.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the core of CircuitSimLab is a <strong>modified-nodal-analysis (MNA)</strong> engine —
        the same approach that powers SPICE. It assembles the system of equations for your circuit
        and solves it for a <strong>DC operating point</strong>, steps it through time for
        <strong>transient analysis</strong>, and sweeps frequency for <strong>AC analysis</strong>.
        There are no fake animations layered on top; the animated current you see is drawn from the
        current the solver actually computed.
      </p>

      <h2>How it is put together</h2>
      <p>
        The whole site is static and served as plain HTML, so pages appear the moment you ask for
        them. The simulator itself is a single interactive island — one focused canvas app rather
        than a page bloated with widgets. Heavy work happens in a
        <strong>Web Worker</strong>, which keeps the solver off the main thread so panning, zooming
        and editing stay smooth even mid-analysis.
      </p>
      <p>
        The engine is deliberately independent of the interface. It is unit-tested on its own terms,
        which means the numbers can be trusted regardless of what the UI happens to be drawing. That
        separation is why we can promise that a reading on the multimeter is a solved result, not a
        prop.
      </p>

      <h2>Where your data lives</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing you build leaves your machine unless you decide it should. Projects are stored in
        your browser with <strong>IndexedDB</strong>, simulations run entirely client-side, and a
        share link encodes the circuit in the URL fragment — which never even reaches a server. No
        account, no tracking, no upload. Privacy here is a consequence of the architecture, not a
        policy bolted on afterward. The details are in our <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-14 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-8 sm:grid-cols-3">
      {principles.map((p) => (
        <div class="border-t border-line pt-4">
          <h3 class="font-semibold">{p.t}</h3>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-ink-2">{p.d}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="border-t border-line bg-surface">
    <div class="container-page py-16 text-center md:py-20">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-3xl">See it solve something.</h2>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-md text-ink-2">
        Open a blank canvas, or start from one of the worked example circuits.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
        <a href="/simulator" class="btn btn-primary text-base">Open the simulator</a>
        <a href="/examples" class="btn btn-ghost text-base">Browse examples</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
